      "content": "I apologize for the earlier confusion; I will focus strictly on the technical risk factors you requested for your report regarding ventilation and fire suppression failures in high-density racking with flammable solvents.\n\n**Ventilation Risks:**\n*   **Positive Pressure Buildup:** In high-density racking, limited air intake can create positive pressure, forcing smoke and toxic fumes from the solvent fire into adjacent aisles or the building's HVAC system, complicating evacuation and firefighting efforts.\n*   **Stack Effect Acceleration:** The vertical nature of tall racking systems acts as a chimney, rapidly drawing oxygen from lower levels to the fire, which can intensify the combustion rate of the solvents far beyond standard fire dynamics.\n*   **Inadequate Exhaust Placement:** If exhaust vents are located too high or in dead zones within the racking bay, they may fail to remove the dense, heavy solvent vapors before they reach the ignition point, leading to flash fires.\n\n**Fire Suppression System Failures:**\n*   **Spray Density vs. Racking Density:** Standard sprinkler systems may not provide sufficient water density to penetrate the deep aisles of high-density racking, leaving pockets of uncooled solvent vapors",
